{"version":"6.9","hitCount":10,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:\"10.1016/S0304-3835(99)00320-1\" OR DOI:\"10.1097/00001648-200101000-00013\" OR TITLE:\"Importance of cell kinetics rhythmicity for control of cell proliferation and carcinogenesis in rat liver\" OR DOI:\"10.1093/jnci/94.9.690\" OR DOI:\"10.1093/carcin/bgi075\" OR DOI:\"10.1016/S0002-9440(10)64135-1\" OR DOI:\"10.1016/S0002-9440(10)65306-0\" OR DOI:\"10.1016/0016-5085(91)90019-H\" OR DOI:\"10.1002/ar.1091200408\" OR DOI:\"10.1074/jbc.M107499200\" OR DOI:\"10.1073/pnas.0502610102\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":20,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"16000406","source":"MED","pmid":"16000406","pmcid":"PMC1177375","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC1177375"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.0502610102","title":"Common pathways in circadian and cell cycle clocks: light-dependent activation of Fos/AP-1 in zebrafish controls CRY-1a and WEE-1.","authorString":"Hirayama J, Cardone L, Doi M, Sassone-Corsi P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hirayama J","lastName":"Hirayama","firstName":"Jun","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8485-3320"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institut de Génétique et de Biologie Moléculaire et Cellulaire, 1, Rue Laurent Fries, 67404 Illkirch, Strasbourg, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Cardone L","lastName":"Cardone","firstName":"Luca","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8184-2856"}},{"fullName":"Doi M","lastName":"Doi","firstName":"Masao","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Sassone-Corsi P","lastName":"Sassone-Corsi","firstName":"Paolo","initials":"P"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8184-2856"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8485-3320"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"29","volume":"102","journalIssueId":1240100,"dateOfPublication":"2005 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2005,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","nlmid":"7505876"},"printPublicationDate":"2005-07-01"},"pubYear":"2005","pageInfo":"10194-10199","abstractText":"The cell cycle and the circadian clock are endogenous pacemakers, which coexist in most eukaryotic cells and share a number of conceptual features. In the zebrafish, light directly regulates the timing of both clocks, although the signaling and transcriptional pathways that convey photic information to essential nuclear regulators have yet to be deciphered. We have previously established the Z3 cell line, which recapitulates the features of zebrafish circadian clock and represents an ideal system to study light-dependent signaling and gene regulation. We conducted a search for light-responsive transcription factors and found that AP-1 DNA binding is highly induced. Light induces the expression of zWee1, a cell cycle gene essential for G2/M transition, and zCry1a, a clock gene of the feedback regulatory loop. We have found consensus AP-1 sites in the regulatory regions of both zWee1 and zCry1a genes, and we show that light inducibility of both genes is abrogated by inhibition of AP-1 function. Light also elicits chromatin remodeling by stimulating hyperacetylation at Lys-14 of histone H3 at both zWee1 and zCry1a promoters, as assessed by chromatin immunoprecipitation assays by using anti-Fos antibody. These findings provide strong evidence that circadian and cell cycle clocks share unique light-responsive pathways in zebrafish.","affiliation":"Institut de Génétique et de Biologie Moléculaire et Cellulaire, 1, Rue Laurent Fries, 67404 Illkirch, Strasbourg, France.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Zebrafish","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Cycle Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Zebrafish Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Flavoproteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nuclear Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transcription Factor AP-1","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Blotting, Western"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Chromatin Immunoprecipitation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cloning, Molecular"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Cycle","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biological Clocks","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Light"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein-Tyrosine Kinases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Promoter Regions, Genetic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cryptochromes"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Nuclear Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Flavoproteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Cell Cycle Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Zebrafish Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Transcription Factor AP-1","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Protein-Tyrosine Kinases","registryNumber":"EC 2.7.10.1"},{"name":"Cryptochromes","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"cry1a protein, zebrafish","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0502610102"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1177375"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1177375?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0502610102"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":77,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2005-10-26","dateOfCreation":"2005-07-08","dateOfRevision":"2018-11-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2005-07-06","firstPublicationDate":"2005-07-06","firstIndexDate":"2009-12-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-02","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"11337377","source":"MED","pmid":"11337377","pmcid":"PMC1891949","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC1891949"]},"doi":"10.1016/s0002-9440(10)64135-1","title":"Circadian expression of clock genes in human oral mucosa and skin: association with specific cell-cycle phases.","authorString":"Bjarnason GA, Jordan RC, Wood PA, Li Q, Lincoln DW, Sothern RB, Hrushesky WJ, Ben-David Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bjarnason GA","lastName":"Bjarnason","firstName":"G A","initials":"GA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6903-5357"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Toronto-Sunnybrook Regional Cancer Centre, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. bjarnason@srcl.sunnybrook.utoronto.ca"}]}},{"fullName":"Jordan RC","lastName":"Jordan","firstName":"R C","initials":"RC"},{"fullName":"Wood PA","lastName":"Wood","firstName":"P A","initials":"PA"},{"fullName":"Li Q","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Q","initials":"Q"},{"fullName":"Lincoln DW","lastName":"Lincoln","firstName":"D W","initials":"DW"},{"fullName":"Sothern RB","lastName":"Sothern","firstName":"R B","initials":"RB"},{"fullName":"Hrushesky WJ","lastName":"Hrushesky","firstName":"W J","initials":"WJ"},{"fullName":"Ben-David Y","lastName":"Ben-David","firstName":"Y","initials":"Y"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6903-5357"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"158","journalIssueId":970421,"dateOfPublication":"2001 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2001,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Am J Pathol","title":"The American journal of pathology","essn":"1525-2191","issn":"0002-9440","isoabbreviation":"Am J Pathol","nlmid":"0370502"},"printPublicationDate":"2001-05-01"},"pubYear":"2001","pageInfo":"1793-1801","abstractText":"We studied the relative RNA expression of clock genes throughout one 24-hour period in biopsies obtained from the oral mucosa and skin from eight healthy diurnally active male study participants. We found that the human clock genes hClock, hTim, hPer1, hCry1, and hBmal1 are expressed in oral mucosa and skin, with a circadian profile consistent with that found in the suprachiasmatic nuclei and the peripheral tissues of rodents. hPer1, hCry1, and hBmal1 have a rhythmic expression, peaking early in the morning, in late afternoon, and at night, respectively, whereas hClock and hTim are not rhythmic. This is the first human study to show a circadian profile of expression for all five clock genes as documented in rodents, suggesting their functional importance in man. In concurrent oral mucosa biopsies, thymidylate synthase enzyme activity, a marker for DNA synthesis, had a circadian variation with peak activity in early afternoon, coinciding with the timing of S phase in our previous study on cell-cycle timing in human oral mucosa. The major peak in hPer1 expression occurs at the same time of day as the peak in G(1) phase in oral mucosa, suggesting a possible link between the circadian clock and the mammalian cell cycle.","affiliation":"Toronto-Sunnybrook Regional Cancer Centre, Toronto, Ontario, Canada. bjarnason@srcl.sunnybrook.utoronto.ca","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Clinical Trial","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA31635","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R01 CA50749","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mouth Mucosa","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Skin","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Thymidylate Synthase","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intracellular Signaling Peptides and Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Cycle Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Trans-Activators","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Eye Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Flavoproteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Drosophila Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Receptors, G-Protein-Coupled"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nuclear Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transcription Factors","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"RNA, Messenger","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Cycle","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Photoreceptor Cells, Invertebrate"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"CLOCK Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"ARNTL Transcription Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cryptochromes"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Period Circadian Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Basic Helix-Loop-Helix Proteins"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"RNA, Messenger","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Eye Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Nuclear Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Drosophila Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Transcription Factors","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Trans-Activators","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Flavoproteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Thymidylate Synthase","registryNumber":"EC 2.1.1.45"},{"name":"Intracellular Signaling Peptides and Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Cell Cycle Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Receptors, G-Protein-Coupled","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"basic helix-loop-helix proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Per1 protein, mouse","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"TIMELESS protein, human","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Timeless protein, mouse","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"PER1 protein, human","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Cryptochromes","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"CLOCK Proteins","registryNumber":"EC 2.3.1.48"},{"name":"Clock protein, mouse","registryNumber":"EC 2.3.1.48"},{"name":"CLOCK protein, human","registryNumber":"EC 2.3.1.48"},{"name":"Period Circadian Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"ARNTL Transcription Factors","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"cry protein, Drosophila","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Bmal1 protein, mouse","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"BMAL1 protein, human","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0002-9440(10)64135-1"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1891949"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1891949?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0002-9440(10)64135-1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":280,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2001-06-21","dateOfCreation":"2001-05-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-28","firstPublicationDate":"2001-05-01","firstIndexDate":"2009-12-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-02","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"10695987","source":"MED","pmid":"10695987","pmcid":"PMC7127503","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC7127503"]},"doi":"10.1016/s0304-3835(99)00320-1","title":"Effect of constant light on DMBA mammary tumorigenesis in rats.","authorString":"Anderson LE, Morris JE, Sasser LB, Stevens RG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Anderson LE","lastName":"Anderson","firstName":"L E","initials":"LE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, Richland, WA 99352, USA. larry.anderson@pnl.gov"}]}},{"fullName":"Morris JE","lastName":"Morris","firstName":"J E","initials":"JE"},{"fullName":"Sasser LB","lastName":"Sasser","firstName":"L B","initials":"LB"},{"fullName":"Stevens RG","lastName":"Stevens","firstName":"R G","initials":"RG"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"148","journalIssueId":1002536,"dateOfPublication":"2000 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2000,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cancer Lett","title":"Cancer letters","essn":"1872-7980","issn":"0304-3835","isoabbreviation":"Cancer Lett","nlmid":"7600053"},"printPublicationDate":"2000-02-01"},"pubYear":"2000","pageInfo":"121-126","abstractText":"A study of light, and mammary tumorigenesis was conducted in rats. One-hundred female Sprague-Dawley rats were divided by weight into two groups. One group was exposed to constant light (LL) from 26 days of age, and the second group was exposed to 8 h light and 16 h dark per day (LD). Both groups received an 8 mg dose of a chemical carcinogen, dimethylben-zanthracene (DMBA) at 52 days of age. At 13 weeks post-DMBA, there were significantly fewer mammary tumors in the LL group compared with the LD group. Constant light was clearly demonstrated to have a profound effect on mammary tissue development. Although virgin, the majority of the LL rats (29/50) had gross evidence of lactation at 141 days of age. None of the LD rats (0/50) showed evidence of milk production. These results suggest that constant light not only substantially accelerated mammary gland development, but pushed development of the tissue past the stage normally observed in virgin animals (to the lactation stage).","affiliation":"Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, Richland, WA 99352, USA. larry.anderson@pnl.gov","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","brief-report","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mammary Glands, Animal","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GD","qualifierName":"growth & development"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rats"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rats, Sprague-Dawley"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mammary Neoplasms, Experimental","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CI","qualifierName":"chemically induced"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cocarcinogenesis"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"9,10-Dimethyl-1,2-benzanthracene"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Carcinogens"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Light"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Darkness"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Carcinogens","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"9,10-Dimethyl-1,2-benzanthracene","registryNumber":"57-97-6"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/pmc7127503?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0304-3835(99)00320-1"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7127503"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC7127503?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":27,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2000-03-21","dateOfCreation":"2000-03-01","dateOfRevision":"2021-01-19","firstPublicationDate":"2000-02-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"10027418","source":"MED","pmid":"10027418","pmcid":"PMC1849996","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC1849996"]},"doi":"10.1016/s0002-9440(10)65306-0","title":"Circadian variation in the expression of cell-cycle proteins in human oral epithelium.","authorString":"Bjarnason GA, Jordan RC, Sothern RB.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bjarnason GA","lastName":"Bjarnason","firstName":"G A","initials":"GA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6903-5357"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Toronto-Sunnybrook Regional Cancer Centre, Department of Medicine, University of Toronto, Ontario, Canada. bjarnason@srcl.sunnybrook.utoronto.ca"}]}},{"fullName":"Jordan RC","lastName":"Jordan","firstName":"R C","initials":"RC"},{"fullName":"Sothern RB","lastName":"Sothern","firstName":"R B","initials":"RB"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6903-5357"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"154","journalIssueId":1191185,"dateOfPublication":"1999 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":1999,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Am J Pathol","title":"The American journal of pathology","essn":"1525-2191","issn":"0002-9440","isoabbreviation":"Am J Pathol","nlmid":"0370502"},"printPublicationDate":"1999-02-01"},"pubYear":"1999","pageInfo":"613-622","abstractText":"At the tissue level, there is experimental and clinical data to suggest a cytokinetic coordination of the cell cycle with a greater proportion of cycling cells entering S-phase and mitosis at specific times of the day. The association of certain cell-cycle proteins with defined events in the cell cycle is well established and may be used to study the timing of cell-cycle phases over 24 hours. In this study oral mucosal biopsies were obtained from six normal human volunteers at 4-hour intervals, six times over 24 hours. Using immunohistochemistry, the number of positive cells expressing the proteins p53, cyclin-E, cyclin-A, cyclin-B1, and Ki-67 was determined for each biopsy and expressed as the number of positive cells per mm of basement membrane. We found a statistically significant circadian variation in the nuclear expression of all of these proteins with the high point of expression for p53 at 10:56 hours, cyclin-E at 14:59 hours, cyclin-A at 16:09 hours, cyclin-B1 at 21:13 hours, and Ki-67 at 02:50 hours. The circadian variation in the nuclear expression of cyclins-E (G1/S phase), -A (G2-phase), and -B1 (M-phase) with a normal physiological progression over time suggests a statistically significant circadian variation in oral epithelial cell proliferation. The finding of a circadian variation in the nuclear expression of p53 protein corresponding to late G1 is novel. This information has clinical implications regarding the timing of chemotherapy and radiotherapy.","affiliation":"Toronto-Sunnybrook Regional Cancer Centre, Department of Medicine, University of Toronto, Ontario, Canada. bjarnason@srcl.sunnybrook.utoronto.ca","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mouth Mucosa","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Epithelial Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cyclins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ki-67 Antigen","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunoenzyme Techniques"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Count"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Tumor Suppressor Protein p53","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Ki-67 Antigen","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Cyclins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Tumor Suppressor Protein p53","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0002-9440(10)65306-0"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1849996"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC1849996?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/s0002-9440(10)65306-0"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":136,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"1999-03-04","dateOfCreation":"1999-02-23","dateOfRevision":"2018-11-13","firstPublicationDate":"1999-02-01","firstIndexDate":"2009-12-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-02","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"15790588","source":"MED","pmid":"15790588","doi":"10.1093/carcin/bgi075","title":"Deregulated expression of the PER1, PER2 and PER3 genes in breast cancers.","authorString":"Chen ST, Choo KB, Hou MF, Yeh KT, Kuo SJ, Chang JG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen ST","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Shou-Tung","initials":"ST","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Molecular Medicine, China Medical University Hospital, Taichung, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Choo KB","lastName":"Choo","firstName":"Kong-Bung","initials":"KB"},{"fullName":"Hou MF","lastName":"Hou","firstName":"Ming-Feng","initials":"MF","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6030-9702"}},{"fullName":"Yeh KT","lastName":"Yeh","firstName":"Kun-Tu","initials":"KT"},{"fullName":"Kuo SJ","lastName":"Kuo","firstName":"Shou-Jen","initials":"SJ"},{"fullName":"Chang JG","lastName":"Chang","firstName":"Jan-Gowth","initials":"JG"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6030-9702"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"26","journalIssueId":1235679,"dateOfPublication":"2005 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2005,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Carcinogenesis","title":"Carcinogenesis","essn":"1460-2180","issn":"0143-3334","isoabbreviation":"Carcinogenesis","nlmid":"8008055"},"printPublicationDate":"2005-07-01"},"pubYear":"2005","pageInfo":"1241-1246","abstractText":"Disruption of circadian rhythm may be a risk factor in the development of breast cancer, but molecular changes in circadian rhythm controlled genes in breast cancer cells are still unexplored. We used immunohistochemical staining, methylation specific PCR and direct sequencing methods to analyze molecular changes in three most important genes, namely PER1, PER2 and PER3, in circadian rhythm in 55 cases of breast cancer of Taiwanese women. Our results reveal disturbances in the expression of the three period (PER) genes in most (>95%) of the breast cancerous cells in comparison with the nearby non-cancerous cells. The PER gene deregulation is not caused by genetic mutations but most probably by methylation of the PER1 or PER2 promoter. Methylation of the PER gene promoters has a strong correlation with c-erbB2 expression (P = 0.017). Since the circadian clock controls expression of cell-cycle related genes, we suggest that disturbances in PER gene expression may result in disruption of the control of the normal circadian clock, thus benefiting the survival of cancer cells and promoting carcinogenesis. Differential expression of circadian genes in non-cancerous and cancerous cells may provide a molecular basis for chronotherapy of breast cancer.","affiliation":"Department of Molecular Medicine, China Medical University Hospital, Taichung, Taiwan.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Breast Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Cycle Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nuclear Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"BI","qualifierName":"biosynthesis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transcription Factors","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"BI","qualifierName":"biosynthesis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Immunohistochemistry"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Profiling"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Polymerase Chain Reaction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Survival"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA Methylation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Regulation, Neoplastic"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged, 80 and over"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Taiwan"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Promoter Regions, Genetic"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Period Circadian Proteins"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Erb-b2 Receptor Tyrosine Kinases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BI","qualifierName":"biosynthesis"}]}}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"PER2 protein, human","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Nuclear Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Transcription Factors","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Cell Cycle Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Per1 protein, mouse","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"PER1 protein, human","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Per3 protein, mouse","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"PER3 protein, human","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Period Circadian Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Erb-b2 Receptor Tyrosine Kinases","registryNumber":"EC 2.7.10.1"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/carcin/bgi075"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":303,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2005-07-29","dateOfCreation":"2005-03-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2005-03-24","firstPublicationDate":"2005-03-24","firstIndexDate":"2009-03-12","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"11983758","source":"MED","pmid":"11983758","doi":"10.1093/jnci/94.9.690","title":"Host circadian clock as a control point in tumor progression.","authorString":"Filipski E, King VM, Li X, Granda TG, Mormont MC, Liu X, Claustrat B, Hastings MH, Lévi F.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Filipski E","lastName":"Filipski","firstName":"Elisabeth","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institut National de la Santé et de la Recherche Médicale, Equipe Propre INSERM 0118 Cancer chronotherapeutics, Université Paris XI, Paul Brousse Hospital, Villejuif, France."}]}},{"fullName":"King VM","lastName":"King","firstName":"Verdun M","initials":"VM"},{"fullName":"Li X","lastName":"Li","firstName":"XiaoMei","initials":"X"},{"fullName":"Granda TG","lastName":"Granda","firstName":"Teresa G","initials":"TG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7490-5932"}},{"fullName":"Mormont MC","lastName":"Mormont","firstName":"Marie-Christine","initials":"MC"},{"fullName":"Liu X","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"XuHui","initials":"X"},{"fullName":"Claustrat B","lastName":"Claustrat","firstName":"Bruno","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Hastings MH","lastName":"Hastings","firstName":"Michael H","initials":"MH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8576-6651"}},{"fullName":"Lévi F","lastName":"Lévi","firstName":"Francis","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1364-7463"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8576-6651"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7490-5932"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1364-7463"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"94","journalIssueId":1075709,"dateOfPublication":"2002 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2002,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Natl Cancer Inst","title":"Journal of the National Cancer Institute","essn":"1460-2105","issn":"0027-8874","isoabbreviation":"J Natl Cancer Inst","nlmid":"7503089"},"printPublicationDate":"2002-05-01"},"pubYear":"2002","pageInfo":"690-697","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The circadian timing system controlled by the suprachiasmatic nuclei (SCN) of the hypothalamus regulates daily rhythms of motor activity and adrenocortical secretion. An alteration in these rhythms is associated with poor survival of patients with metastatic colorectal or breast cancer. We developed a mouse model to investigate the consequences of severe circadian dysfunction upon tumor growth.<h4>Methods</h4>The SCN of mice were destroyed by bilateral electrolytic lesions, and body activity and body temperature were recorded with a radio transmitter implanted into the peritoneal cavity. Plasma corticosterone levels and circulating lymphocyte counts were measured (n = 75 with SCN lesions, n = 64 sham-operated). Complete SCN destruction was ascertained postmortem. Mice were inoculated with implants of Glasgow osteosarcoma (n = 16 with SCN lesions, n = 12 sham-operated) or pancreatic adenocarcinoma (n = 13 with SCN lesions, n = 13 sham-operated) tumors to determine the effects of altered circadian rhythms on tumor progression. Time series for body temperature and rest-activity patterns were analyzed by spectral analysis and cosinor analysis. Parametric data were compared by the use of analysis of variance (ANOVA) and survival curves with the log-rank test. All statistical tests were two-sided.<h4>Results</h4>The 24-hour rest-activity cycle was ablated and the daily rhythms of serum corticosterone level and lymphocyte count were markedly altered in 75 mice with complete SCN destruction as compared with 64 sham-operated mice (two-way ANOVA for corticosterone: sampling time effect P<.001, lesion effect P =.001, and time x lesion interaction P<.001; for lymphocytes P =.001,.002, and.002 respectively). Body temperature rhythm was suppressed in 60 of the 75 mice with SCN lesions (P<.001). Both types of tumors grew two to three times faster in mice with SCN lesions than in sham-operated mice (two-way ANOVA: P<.001 for lesion and for tumor effects; P =.21 for lesion x tumor effect interaction). Survival of mice with SCN lesions was statistically significantly shorter compared with that of sham-operated mice (log-rank P =.0062).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Disruption of circadian rhythms in mice was associated with accelerated growth of malignant tumors of two types, suggesting that the host circadian clock may play an important role in endogenous control of tumor progression.","affiliation":"Institut National de la Santé et de la Recherche Médicale, Equipe Propre INSERM 0118 Cancer chronotherapeutics, Université Paris XI, Paul Brousse Hospital, Villejuif, France.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Suprachiasmatic Nucleus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"SU","qualifierName":"surgery"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice, Inbred C57BL"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Osteosarcoma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adenocarcinoma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Skin Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disease Models, Animal"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Body Weight"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Corticosterone","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BL","qualifierName":"blood"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Organ Size"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Body Temperature"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Lymphocyte Count"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Corticosterone","registryNumber":"W980KJ009P"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jnci/article-pdf/94/9/690/9849347/690.pdf"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jnci/94.9.690"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":310,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2002-05-22","dateOfCreation":"2002-05-02","dateOfRevision":"2022-03-11","firstPublicationDate":"2002-05-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"11598123","source":"MED","pmid":"11598123","doi":"10.1074/jbc.m107499200","title":"Circadian regulation of diverse gene products revealed by mRNA expression profiling of synchronized fibroblasts.","authorString":"Grundschober C, Delaunay F, Pühlhofer A, Triqueneaux G, Laudet V, Bartfai T, Nef P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Grundschober C","lastName":"Grundschober","firstName":"C","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0190-9051"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Central Nervous System Department, F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd., CH-4070 Basel, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Delaunay F","lastName":"Delaunay","firstName":"F","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4927-1701"}},{"fullName":"Pühlhofer A","lastName":"Pühlhofer","firstName":"A","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Triqueneaux G","lastName":"Triqueneaux","firstName":"G","initials":"G"},{"fullName":"Laudet V","lastName":"Laudet","firstName":"V","initials":"V"},{"fullName":"Bartfai T","lastName":"Bartfai","firstName":"T","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Nef P","lastName":"Nef","firstName":"P","initials":"P"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0190-9051"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4927-1701"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","full_text","related_data","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"50","volume":"276","journalIssueId":1035130,"dateOfPublication":"2001 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2001,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biol Chem","title":"The Journal of biological chemistry","essn":"1083-351X","issn":"0021-9258","isoabbreviation":"J Biol Chem","nlmid":"2985121R"},"printPublicationDate":"2001-12-01"},"pubYear":"2001","pageInfo":"46751-46758","abstractText":"Genes under a 24-h regulation period may represent drug targets relevant to diseases involving circadian dysfunctions. As a testing model of the circadian clock system, we have used synchronized rat fibroblasts that are known to express at least six genes in a circadian fashion. We have determined the expression patterns of 9957 transcripts every 4 h over a total period of 76 h using high density oligonucleotide microarrays. The spectral analysis of our mRNA profiling data indicated that approximately 2% (85 genes) of all expressed genes followed a robust circadian pattern. We have confirmed the circadian expression of previously known clock or clock-driven genes, and we identified 81 novel circadian genes. The majority of the circadian-regulated gene products are known and are involved in diverse cellular functions. We have classified these circadian genes in seven clusters according to their phase of cycling. Our pathway analysis of the mRNA profiling data strongly suggests a direct link between circadian rhythm and cell cycle.","affiliation":"Central Nervous System Department, F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd., CH-4070 Basel, Switzerland.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Fibroblasts","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rats"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ribonucleases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA, Complementary","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"RNA, Messenger","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Oligonucleotide Array Sequence Analysis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Expression Profiling","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Reverse Transcriptase Polymerase Chain Reaction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Kinetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Expressed Sequence Tags"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Time Factors"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"RNA, Messenger","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Ribonucleases","registryNumber":"EC 3.1.-"},{"name":"DNA, Complementary","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M107499200"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":93,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2002-01-24","dateOfCreation":"2001-10-13","dateOfRevision":"2021-02-09","electronicPublicationDate":"2001-10-11","firstPublicationDate":"2001-10-11","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"11138824","source":"MED","pmid":"11138824","doi":"10.1097/00001648-200101000-00013","title":"Increased breast cancer risk among women who work predominantly at night.","authorString":"Hansen J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hansen J","lastName":"Hansen","firstName":"J","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9342-2725"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Danish Cancer Society, Institute of Cancer Epidemiology, Copenhagen."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9342-2725"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"12","journalIssueId":944129,"dateOfPublication":"2001 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2001,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Epidemiology","title":"Epidemiology (Cambridge, Mass.)","essn":"1531-5487","issn":"1044-3983","isoabbreviation":"Epidemiology","nlmid":"9009644"},"printPublicationDate":"2001-01-01"},"pubYear":"2001","pageInfo":"74-77","abstractText":"Irregular working hours, including working at night, have serious psychological and physiological effects. In a nationwide population-based case-control study, we investigated the breast cancer risk among 30- to 54-year-old Danish women who worked predominantly at night. Individual employment histories were reconstructed back to 1964 for each of 7035 women with breast cancer and their individually matched controls from the records of a nationwide pension scheme with compulsory membership. Odds ratios, including 5 years of induction time and adjusted for socio-economic status, age at the birth of first and last child and number of children, were estimated by conditional logistic regression analysis. The odds ratio for breast cancer among women who worked at night at least half of a year was 1.5 (95% confidence interval, 1.2 to 1.7), and there was a tendency to increasing odds ratio by increasing duration of nighttime employment.","affiliation":"Danish Cancer Society, Institute of Cancer Epidemiology, Copenhagen.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Breast Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Odds Ratio"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Factors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Case-Control Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Work Schedule Tolerance"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Employment"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Personnel Staffing and Scheduling"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Denmark","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Unpaywall","url":"https://journals.lww.com/epidem/Fulltext/2001/01000/Increased_Breast_Cancer_Risk_among_Women_Who_Work.13.aspx"},{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/00001648-200101000-00013"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"11505182","reference":"Epidemiology. 2001 Sep;12(5):588-9. doi: 10.1097/00001648-200109000-00023","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":291,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2001-02-15","dateOfCreation":"2001-01-04","dateOfRevision":"2022-04-08","firstPublicationDate":"2001-01-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"2065918","source":"MED","pmid":"2065918","doi":"10.1016/0016-5085(91)90019-h","title":"Circadian rhythm of cellular proliferation in the human rectal mucosa.","authorString":"Buchi KN, Moore JG, Hrushesky WJ, Sothern RB, Rubin NH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Buchi KN","lastName":"Buchi","firstName":"K N","initials":"KN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, University of Utah School of Medicine, Salt Lake City."}]}},{"fullName":"Moore JG","lastName":"Moore","firstName":"J G","initials":"JG"},{"fullName":"Hrushesky WJ","lastName":"Hrushesky","firstName":"W J","initials":"WJ"},{"fullName":"Sothern RB","lastName":"Sothern","firstName":"R B","initials":"RB"},{"fullName":"Rubin NH","lastName":"Rubin","firstName":"N H","initials":"NH"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"101","journalIssueId":852103,"dateOfPublication":"1991 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":1991,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Gastroenterology","title":"Gastroenterology","essn":"1528-0012","issn":"0016-5085","isoabbreviation":"Gastroenterology","nlmid":"0374630"},"printPublicationDate":"1991-08-01"},"pubYear":"1991","pageInfo":"410-415","abstractText":"Circadian rhythms of DNA synthesis and cellular proliferation in the gastrointestinal mucosa have been well documented in animal models. This investigation was designed to determine whether similar rhythms could be demonstrated in the human rectal epithelium: 24 studies were performed in 16 healthy men under fasting (n = 14) and fed (n = 10) conditions. Rectal mucosal biopsy specimens were obtained through a proctoscope every 2 or 3 hours for a 24-hour span. Ex vivo measurements of tritiated thymidine incorporation into DNA were made on the mucosal samples. Feeding and time of day were each found to have an effect on the rate of thymidine incorporation into the DNA of rectal mucosal cells. Both fasted and fed subjects showed significant circadian rhythms in thymidine incorporation, which peaked at about 7 AM. Fasting lowered the overall mean thymidine uptake without altering the rhythm. Thymidine uptake generally reflects the amount of DNA synthesis in the sampled tissue. Therefore, these data may be important in the design of cancer chemotherapeutic regimens that use drugs specifically active during DNA synthesis.","affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, University of Utah School of Medicine, Salt Lake City.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"PHS HHS","grantId":"R01-31635-06","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intestinal Mucosa","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rectum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"BI","qualifierName":"biosynthesis"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Biopsy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Analysis of Variance"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Division","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Circadian Rhythm","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"}]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"DNA","registryNumber":"9007-49-2"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/0016-5085(91)90019-h"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":112,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"1991-08-15","dateOfCreation":"1991-08-01","dateOfRevision":"2019-07-07","firstPublicationDate":"1991-08-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"14350262","source":"MED","pmid":"14350262","doi":"10.1002/ar.1091200408","title":"Diurnal mitotic periodicity in regenerating rat liver.","authorString":"JAFFE JJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"JAFFE JJ","lastName":"JAFFE","firstName":"J J","initials":"JJ"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"120","journalIssueId":148205,"dateOfPublication":"1954 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":1954,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Anat Rec","title":"The Anatomical record","essn":"1097-0185","issn":"0003-276X","isoabbreviation":"Anat Rec","nlmid":"0370540"},"printPublicationDate":"1954-12-01"},"pubYear":"1954","pageInfo":"935-954","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Liver","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Rats"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cell Division"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Periodicity"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Digestion"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cell division","Periodicity","Liver/physiology"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"OM","name":"pre-1966 citations"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/ar.1091200408"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":79,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2003-05-01","dateOfCreation":"1954-12-01","dateOfRevision":"2019-09-10","firstPublicationDate":"1954-12-01","firstIndexDate":"2008-01-29","hasEvaluations":"N"}]}}